pivot_table()

pivot_table() is more powerful because it can aggregate data using functions such as:

sum
mean
count
min
max

Syntax:

df.pivot_table(
    index="row_column",
    columns="column_column",
    values="value_column",
    aggfunc="sum"
)

===

Real-time example

Imagine a company has multiple sales transactions:

data = {
    "Name": ["John", "John", "John", "Mary", "Mary"],
    "Product": ["Laptop", "Laptop", "Mobile", "Laptop", "Mobile"],
    "Sales": [50000, 30000, 20000, 60000, 25000]
}

df = pd.DataFrame(data)

print(df)

====

| Name | Product |  Sales |
| ---- | ------- | -----: |
| John | Laptop  | 50,000 |
| John | Laptop  | 30,000 |
| John | Mobile  | 20,000 |
| Mary | Laptop  | 60,000 |
| Mary | Mobile  | 25,000 |


====

There are two Laptop transactions for John.

If we use pivot():

df.pivot(
    index="Name",
    columns="Product",
    values="Sales"
)

❌ This produces an error because John has duplicate Name + Product combinations.

Instead, use:

result = df.pivot_table(
    index="Name",
    columns="Product",
    values="Sales",
    aggfunc="sum"
)

print(result)

======

Result

| Name | Laptop | Mobile |
| ---- | -----: | -----: |
| John | 80,000 | 20,000 |
| Mary | 60,000 | 25,000 |

John's Laptop sales:

50,000 + 30,000 = 80,000

